   "keyframes_note": "Every bridge clip is cover-fitted first (Motion / layer Scale), then the Transform effect resizes it inside its own frame: scale % = contain-fit / cover-fit. After Effects Position is in the SOURCE's pixels: (frame point - frame centre) / cover scale + source centre, which is simply frame point / cover scale when the source has the frame's aspect; Premiere's is a frame fraction. A 16:9 source in 1080x1920 cover-fits at 177.8% (1920 px tall); contain-fit is 56.25%, so the foreground copy's Transform scale is 31.64%. The background copy: Gaussian Blur (the same engine in both apps: AE ADBE Gaussian Blur 2 = Premiere 'Gaussian Blur (Legacy)'), blurred BEFORE a 110% Transform so the soft edge band is pushed out of frame, then Brightness -35. The blur is in source pixels and the layer is then scaled 177.8%, so 25 reads as about 44 px. Both copies have the same in point and start so they stay in sync; the copy on track 1 carries the clip's audio too (mute one: the bridge places both). Measured 2026-10-13: Premiere's Brightness & Contrast renders this backplate 10-27 levels darker than After Effects' at the same values (9:16 fill mean RGB Premiere 104/103/105, After Effects 126/115/127); match the two backplates by eye, not by copying the values.",
